    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p14_b10",
      "text": "To verify that performance gains are driven by topology\nquality rather than superficial features, we compare three\ngraph extraction configurations: (1) a weak extractor that\nproduces smaller, less-structured graphs via simpler prompt-\ning or weaker parsing; (2) the default extractor described\nin Section 2; and (3) a strong extractor that uses chain-of-\nthought prompting and cross-validation to produce more\ncomplete and accurate graphs. Table 18 shows that win-rate\nand structural coherence track extractor quality. The weak\nextractor yields only modest gains over vanilla DPO, while\nthe strong extractor achieves the best results. Importantly,\nthese improvements are not explained by graph size alone:\nthe weak extractor produces graphs with comparable node\ncounts but lower path coverage and higher contradiction\nrates, resulting in weaker performance.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.13,
        409.78,
        291.1,
        587.2
      ],
